## Guard rails follow the policy

A rule the project's tools do not enforce is not a guard rail. For every rule a finding names, and whenever a policy rule is added, restored, or tightened, change the enforcing tool configuration in this same pass: enable the project's own lint, type-check, or audit rule at the policy's value, or add the audit or gate step that enforces it, for whatever languages and tools the project uses. Name that enforcing command in the guard's Enforcement cell, and prove it: show the command fails on a seeded violation and passes once the violation is removed. A correct guard rail that reports existing product violations is the build working: leave those violations for development, record them as tracked work with a dated review trigger, and report them as residual blockers. Never make the build pass by disabling a rule, raising a limit, excluding a path, rewording the policy to match the code, or editing product code or tests.

Policy evidence must be durable: replace every citation of a worktree-local or append-only record (`tmp/`, `.agent/`, a log, an `*.ndjson` ledger) by line, count, or excerpt with the stage name and a dated, durable source; never re-point or recount it, and never keep a receipt that exists in only one checkout.
If a check that reads the policy corpus fails because it requires those citations, replace that check's oracle with a run-time derivation from the local record (testing-policy.md rule 29 names this case), editing that check as part of this remediation; never restore the citations to satisfy it, and never check out, revert or restore a policy file to make a gate pass. A gate that stays red for that reason is reported as a residual blocker, not fixed by undoing the policy.

## Work from evidence

